            {
                "entry_id": "3d011157-3c7b-3239-a788-ab5eb1696533",
                "word": "Quietism",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5824ba8f-a32c-3951-9e91-7ff950f41959",
                        "entry_id": "3d011157-3c7b-3239-a788-ab5eb1696533",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of quietism (“form of mysticism”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "be918b9a-c865-401a-a5fc-a603ce1dc75a",
                                "sense_id": "5824ba8f-a32c-3951-9e91-7ff950f41959",
                                "text": "In its consistently logical form Quietism makes communion between man and God an impossibility by annulling the distinction between them, ultimately reducing God to a vague and empty abstraction, and dehumanizing man."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5c6cc7f-bac9-4fc3-aace-4dcbcac9f40d",
                                "sense_id": "5824ba8f-a32c-3951-9e91-7ff950f41959",
                                "text": "In von Hügel's estimation the real culprit that distorts “quiet” into Quietism, is any tendency to extreme dualism, particularly between body and soul."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5ea4a0ff-b3b0-465c-af10-371b1d8c4103",
                                "sense_id": "5824ba8f-a32c-3951-9e91-7ff950f41959",
                                "text": "The problem of Quietism or rather the problem of Quietism in its most corrupted forms and their immediate repercussions on a disciplinary, moral and sexual level led to the realization that the dangers that believers faced were as much inside the Church as outside."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
